- op: discuss  # 797d
  at: '2026-05-01T19:39:18Z'  # 797d
  by: agent  # 797d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 797d
  clock: 2  # 797d
  nonce: 797d  # 797d
  message: "AUDIT VERDICT: DEPRECATE — full ADR-0023-shape migration warranted. Confirms the ADR-0023 Open Question #2 prediction ('probably needs its own ADR'). Filing follow-on ADR draft as a separate item.\n\nINVENTORY: 210 distinct evidence_type values found in production code (lowercase identifier-shaped string literals at evidence_type= sites under packages/, excluding tests).\n\nCLUSTER TAXONOMY — three axes conflated:\n\nCLUSTER A — INFERENCE PATHWAY (the documented purpose of the field): The values that actually answer 'how did we conclude this edge exists?'. ~75-90 values: ast_call_direct, ast_import, ast_extends, ast_new, ast_attribute, ast_implements, ast_decorator, ast_annotation, ast_call_static, ast_call_type_inferred, ast_method_this, ast_method_inferred, ast_type_ref, tree_sitter, naming_convention, scip_occurrence_ref, function_call, method_call, import_statement, import_declaration, include, require, constructor_call, object_creation, function_reference, function_reference_arg, callable_reference, function_pointer, function_pointer_arg, dispatch_table_initializer, dispatch_table_reference, struct_field_reference, object_field_reference, module_attribute_reference, method_reference, function_application, typed_receiver_call, typed_field_call, receiver_call, bare_method_call, pipe_call, ambiguous_method_call, method_call_type_inferred, method_call_field_chain, stdlib_method_call, method_call_recovery, span_overlap, canonical_name, alias_resolution, import_resolution, hg_annotation, type_hierarchy, behaviour_callback, closure_wrapper, build_target_main, import_to_manifest, bridging_header_import.\n\nCLUSTER B — RESOLUTION-STATUS smuggled in: Every \\*_unresolved / unresolved_\\* variant doubles as a resolution-status flag. 'ast_annotation' vs 'ast_annotation_unresolved' answers BOTH 'inference pathway' AND 'did we resolve the target?' in the same field. 6+ values: unresolved_external_call, unresolved_method_call, ast_annotation_unresolved, ast_decorator_unresolved, luajit_ffi_unresolved, grpc_unresolved_resolution. **This is the canonical Test 4 (mechanism vs category) failure: a flag dimension squeezed into a label.**\n\nCLUSTER C — FRAMEWORK-SPECIFIC DISPATCH CONVENTIONS: The third axis. 30+ values: django_orm_dispatch, airflow_framework_dispatch, jackson_bean_dispatch, kafka_streams_dispatch, otp_genserver_dispatch, rust_trait_dispatch, go_cobra_dispatch, dispatch_pattern, nestjs_module_registration, controller_routes, router_routes, middleware_chain, ruby_delegate, activerecord_association, orm_accessor_pattern, phoenix_event_match, resolver_field_match, resolver_type_match, graphql_operation_match, openapi_path_match, openapi_operation_id_match, vue_event_handler, vue_component_import, yjs_crdt_pattern, implicit_convention, registry_dispatch, job_enqueue, struct_field_reference, signal_receiver_match, http_url_match, table_name_match, crypto_api_pattern, subprocess_cli_match, tauri_invoke, tauri_emit_listen, event_name_match, specta_wrapper_import, jni_naming_convention, abi_name_match.\n\nCLUSTER D — APEX/PEER OVERLOADS (Test 2 fires): function_call (38 occurrences) coexists with method_call (6), function_application (3), local_call, remote_call, typed_receiver_call, ambiguous_method_call, ... — multiple flavors of 'a call happened, here's how we noticed' in the same field. 'call' as apex of every \\*_call peer.\n\nFOUR LEAKAGE TESTS (cluster-level):\n\n- Test 1 (Property derivability): Cluster B values (resolution status) are derivable from a separate boolean: 'is the dst symbol resolved?' — easily a sibling Edge field. Cluster C values (framework conventions) duplicate information that Edge.meta['framework'] / meta['protocol'] already carries. **MASSIVE LEAKAGE.**\n\n- Test 2 (Apex/peer): function_call vs method_call vs function_application vs typed_receiver_call vs ambiguous_method_call — same fundamental relationship (a call) split across N variants by inference path detail. 'function_call' is sometimes apex, sometimes peer of method_call. **FIRES.**\n\n- Test 3 (Construct vs relationship): Cluster A members like 'ast_extends' name a syntactic construct ('an extends keyword was lexed'); Cluster B members like 'unresolved' name a resolution outcome. Different categories of question. **FIRES.**\n\n- Test 4 (Mechanism vs category): Cluster C — django_orm_dispatch is a MECHANISM (HOW the framework dispatched); the analyzer's category-question is just 'a call edge'. **FIRES.**\n\nFOLLOW-ON ADR SCOPE (next-stage tracker item):\n\nThe migration mirrors ADR-0023's playbook, instantiating ADR-0024:\n\n1. CANONICAL AXIS: 'inference pathway' — the value names HOW the analyzer concluded this edge exists. Cluster A is the seed (after de-overload — collapsing function_call / method_call / function_application to canonical 'ast_call' + meta['call_construct'] = direct/method/typed/ambiguous).\n\n2. SIBLING FIELD: introduce Edge.is_resolved: bool (or resolution_status: enum) carrying Cluster B's information. Cluster B values fold to Cluster A canonical + the new sibling.\n\n3. META MIGRATION: Cluster C values fold to Cluster A canonical + meta['framework_dispatch'] or meta['detection_pattern'] — same shape ADR-0026/ADR-0025 used for IPC and dispatch families.\n\n4. APEX/PEER COLLAPSE: function_call / method_call / function_application / typed_receiver_call / ambiguous_method_call all collapse to canonical 'ast_call' (or 'function_call' if that's the chosen apex) + meta['call_construct'] in {direct, method, typed_method, ambiguous, ...}.\n\n5. PHASES: registry + drift linter (Phase 1; reuse axis_drift.find_drift with name_filter='EVIDENCE_TYPE'); consumer migration (Phase 2; consumers query (evidence_type, is_resolved, meta['framework_dispatch'])); producer migration (Phase 3; ~70-100 emit sites); schema bump (Phase 4).\n\n6. ESTIMATED BLAST RADIUS: larger than ADR-0023's because every analyzer touches evidence_type. ~140 production files have evidence_type literals. Producer-rename Phase 3 will likely take 7-12 days plus bakeoff validation.\n\nThis is the largest concept-axis migration the project has on its roadmap. Worth flagging as 'this is going to be the next-most-complex multi-PR migration of the year' when ADR-0028 lands."  # 797d
